INV-virat -- the one-slot last-writer-wins class, THIRD instance in two days,
filed and fixed as the CLASS. Prior instances: INV-zumin (two catalogue rows
true at once; row order discarded one) and INV-pojib's sanitizer registry (one
slot per (caller,label); last registered overwrote the rest).

THE DEFECT. tag_io_boundaries assigned meta['io_boundary'] unconditionally:

    before: {'io_boundary': 'command_launch'}          # bash.py:534 stamp
    after:  {'io_boundary': 'fs_read', 'io_primitive': 'os.listdir'}

command_launch is the analyzer's opacity observation -- "control left this
process for a program I cannot see" -- and the ONLY such evidence those edges
will ever carry (ADR-0016 rules out a bash catalogue). Any matching row
destroyed it.

WHY NO FALSE CONFIRM SHIPPED, STATED HONESTLY: the opacity gate
(_opaque_launch_sites, INV-larol) reads raw_edges -- the serialized dicts --
while the tagger mutates objects whose meta _rehydrate_io_boundary_edges
happens to SHALLOW-COPY for an unrelated reason (WI-kumol: preserving
is_resolved/dst_ref). The safety property rested on read order plus an
accidental copy. A reasonable refactor -- sharing the meta dict to save an
allocation per edge -- would have silently restored the INV-larol false
confirm with every test green, since no test constructs edges through
rehydration. Reachability today is two accidents deep (no bash catalogue
possible + overlays inert for uncatalogued languages, WI-guhuv -- an OPEN item
whose fix removes that guard), so this is prevention landed before the arming
event, the same ordering WI-joluk's gate was built under.

THE FIX IS THE INV-zumin MECHANISM WITH A SECOND WRITER. curl -o
/etc/cron.d/pwned IS two facts at once -- a net_send and an opaque launch --
which is exactly what `io_boundaries` was built for; the second writer here is
an ANALYZER rather than another row. The producer stamp stays PRIMARY
(io_boundary): the analyzer SAW the launch, the catalogue merely ASSERTS the
I/O -- different trust properties, now documented at the write site. The
catalogue view lands additively (io_boundaries + io_primitive).

CHAIN ACCOUNTING keeps both facts under their own rules, pinned by test: the
launch chain stays disclosed-only (command_launch_edges, excluded from
total_io_edges) exactly as bash launches are today; the catalogue-boundary
chain is counted. One edge, two chains, no double-count.

MEASURED END-TO-END with a temporary curl->net_send bash row (removed by
absolute path after the probe; the earlier relative-path cleanup miss is why
that is called out):

    fs_write claim:  inconclusive rc 2        (opacity HELD with the row present)
    boundary map:    command_launch {curl.curl: 1}  AND  net_send {curl.curl: 1}
                     command_launch_edges=1, total_io_edges=1

Pre-fix, the tagger destroyed the stamp on the tagged objects, so the map
carried only the net_send half.

NON-DESTRUCTION, re-run live: INV-zumin's five-arm probe byte-identical
(scala/objc dual chains; open/unistd.write/os.listdir single-tagged), and
WI-jupaf's erlang subprocess claim still violated rc 1. An unstamped edge tags
exactly as before, no new key. INV-larol's read-order gate is KEPT as a belt
-- cheap, independent, and the two mechanisms fail differently.

